// @ARGS: --lib tests/lib
//
// The ENTRY file's `main` is the one that runs, even when an imported module declares one
// too (loft#1351).
//
// `Data` scopes definitions by (name, source), but emitted Rust is one flat namespace, so
// `namespace_colliding_native_fns` renames all but the lowest source's copy to
// `n_s<N>_<name>`.  Its contract is that it runs after the two-pass parse and BEFORE a
// native emit, and every `--native` path in `main.rs` calls it.  `tests/native.rs` did
// not, so the harness emitted from a `Data` no real build produces: two definitions named
// `n_main`, which `duplicate_fn_names` then reported as a collision that does not exist,
// so BOTH were emitted under a disambiguated ident (`n_main_m0b5dfab1`) while the entry
// template called the bare `n_main`.  rustc: `cannot find function n_main in this scope`.
//
// The control is in the module rather than here: `entrylib`'s own `main` asserts false, so
// choosing the wrong entry FAILS instead of quietly running nothing. That matters because
// the wrong-entry outcome is "the assertions never ran", which reads like a pass.
//
// @falsified-at: d47eec4c — native `1 compile failed` -> 0; the file did not build at all
// on the pre-fix harness (`error[E0425]: cannot find function n_main`), so the interpret
// side passed while the native side could not produce a binary.  The narrowed entry LOOKUP
// added in that same commit is not enough on its own: it picks the right definition and
// the emit still fails, because the duplicate name is what drives the mangling.
//   CHANNEL: the NATIVE BUILD — `1 compile failed` -> 0. On the pre-fix harness the file did
//   not build at all (`error[E0425]: cannot find function n_main`), so the interpret side
//   passed while the native side could not produce a binary.
//   HOLDS: the interpret side is equal throughout and cannot carry this. A sweep that scores
//   `--interpret` reads the guard as inert, and a whole-tree verdict reads `here-not-clean`
//   because the native leg is the subject rather than a program value.

use entrylib;

fn main() {
  assert(entrylib::entry_value() == 41, "the module is reachable: {entrylib::entry_value()}");
}
